[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186129318 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/id #uuid "7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/math-academy-id "q-138973" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer-field/choices ["answer-45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" "answer-c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" "answer-053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" "answer-53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" "answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" :answer/id #uuid "45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" :answer/id #uuid "c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" :answer/id #uuid "053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" :answer/id #uuid "53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/id #uuid "d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186396318 :question/problem "Consider a function $b(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{b(t)\\}=B(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186172103 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" :question/id #uuid "ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-139125" :question/problem "The displacement $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your final answer to the nearest Newton." :question/worked-solution "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot ⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18t,\\,36{t}^{2}-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =⟨18(4),\\,36({4}^{2})-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨72,\\,570⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F(4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(4)| & =\\sqrt{(72)^{2}+{(570)}^{2}} \\\\  & ≈575\\,\\text{N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest Newton." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/id #uuid "31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea" "answer-beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f" "answer-9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787" "answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" "answer-8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea" :answer/id #uuid "87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "430\\,\\text{N}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f" :answer/id #uuid "beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "491\\,\\text{N}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787" :answer/id #uuid "9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "511\\,\\text{N}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" :answer/id #uuid "c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "575\\,\\text{N}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d" :answer/id #uuid "8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "612\\,\\text{N}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186411821 :answer-field/correct "answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab" :answer/id #uuid "d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2(t-2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064315 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/id #uuid "2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143507" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{75}$ be a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75,$ calculate $P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =75,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.5,\\frac{0.75}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.5,0.01).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6) & =P(\\frac{1.5-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.01}}<Z<\\frac{1.6-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.01}}) \\\\  & =P(0<Z<1) \\\\  & =P(Z<1)-P(Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(1)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1)=0.8413,\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution, $Φ(0)=0.5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6) & =Φ(1)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =0.8413-0.5 \\\\  & =0.3413.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8" "answer-7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7" "answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" "answer-b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6" "answer-e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8" :answer/id #uuid "0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3159" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7" :answer/id #uuid "7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3212" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" :answer/id #uuid "654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3413" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6" :answer/id #uuid "b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3461" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd" :answer/id #uuid "e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3665" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186398726 :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by two units to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-2))=u(2-t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(2-t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(2-t).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}] "assertions" [["question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/id #uuid "7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750"] ["question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/math-academy-id "q-138973"] ["question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y={sin}^{2}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?"] ["question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\pi .$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t(1+{cos}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot |2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6"] ["answer-45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" :answer/id #uuid "45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff"] ["answer-45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff" :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{4+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["answer-c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" :answer/id #uuid "c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c"] ["answer-c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c" :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{4}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["answer-053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" :answer/id #uuid "053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076"] ["answer-053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076" :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["answer-53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" :answer/id #uuid "53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227"] ["answer-53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227" :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t"] ["answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/id #uuid "d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016"] ["answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016" :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{3}⁡t\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t"] ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/correct "answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016"] ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/choices "answer-45595bd3-a3a8-53e3-bf8d-e8a743e2acff"] ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/choices "answer-c04ad9e8-50e3-5c90-8826-dc328c55a80c"] ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/choices "answer-053308a9-7001-5846-9b1c-aebce9551076"] ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/choices "answer-53f5cfc8-bae2-544a-87f1-7473d053b227"] ["answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6" :answer-field/choices "answer-d0da0530-4038-57b2-ad9c-a85207241016"] ["question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-bc195312-1e76-599e-a6d3-b22fd38f25b6"] [17592186129318 :knowledge-point/questions "question-7a3648d6-4396-5f3b-a4a5-7bdad067d750"] [17592186396318 :question/problem "Consider a function $b(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{b(t)\\}=B(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nBy definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" :question/id #uuid "ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08"] ["question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-139125"] ["question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" :question/problem "The displacement $r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨{t}^{3},\\,{t}^{4}-{t}^{2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the magnitude of the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$ Round your final answer to the nearest Newton."] ["question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" :question/worked-solution "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,4{t}^{3}-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot ⟨6t,\\,12{t}^{2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨18t,\\,36{t}^{2}-6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=4\\,\\text{s},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(4) & =⟨18(4),\\,36({4}^{2})-6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨72,\\,570⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of $F(4)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|F(4)| & =\\sqrt{(72)^{2}+{(570)}^{2}} \\\\  & ≈575\\,\\text{N}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest Newton."] ["question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/id #uuid "31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166"] ["answer-87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea" :answer/id #uuid "87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea"] ["answer-87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea" :answer/value "430\\,\\text{N}"] ["answer-beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f" :answer/id #uuid "beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f"] ["answer-beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f" :answer/value "491\\,\\text{N}"] ["answer-9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787" :answer/id #uuid "9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787"] ["answer-9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787" :answer/value "511\\,\\text{N}"] ["answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" :answer/id #uuid "c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f"] ["answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f" :answer/value "575\\,\\text{N}"] ["answer-8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d" :answer/id #uuid "8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d"] ["answer-8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d" :answer/value "612\\,\\text{N}"] ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/correct "answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f"] ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/choices "answer-87807497-9d16-516b-a885-3af2d03156ea"] ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/choices "answer-beccc26a-d2bc-5b22-9004-128752f9150f"] ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/choices "answer-9653eea3-31da-5fdd-992a-434023dc2787"] ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/choices "answer-c6aedca6-99db-59f9-95e2-e9fdad4e753f"] ["answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166" :answer-field/choices "answer-8965d7e6-0eda-5e94-9abc-17673bfc7c7d"] ["question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-31c651ea-1589-526b-91c7-bd4b6db7a166"] [17592186172103 :knowledge-point/questions "question-ac6c0c1f-be4b-5795-8838-93be63fc1a08"] ["answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab" :answer/id #uuid "d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab"] ["answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab" :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2(t-2)}"] [17592186411821 :answer-field/correct "answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab"] [17592186411821 :answer-field/choices "answer-d8681e3a-87bd-5430-9dea-17ec9e0977ab"] ["question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/id #uuid "2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d"] ["question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/math-academy-id "q-143507"] ["question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/problem "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{75}$ be a random sample of size $n=75$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75,$ calculate $P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8413\\,$ | $\\,0.8438\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8643\\,$ | $\\,0.8665\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ |"] ["question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =75,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{3}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{3}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{3}{2{x}^{2}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }[\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{3}{2{b}^{2}}] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =1.5,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.75.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.5,\\frac{0.75}{75}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.5,0.01).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6) & =P(\\frac{1.5-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.01}}<Z<\\frac{1.6-1.5}{\\sqrt{0.01}}) \\\\  & =P(0<Z<1) \\\\  & =P(Z<1)-P(Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(1)-Φ(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(1)=0.8413,\n$$\n\nand by the symmetry of the normal distribution, $Φ(0)=0.5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1.5<\\overset{―}{X}<1.6) & =Φ(1)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =0.8413-0.5 \\\\  & =0.3413.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1"] ["answer-0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8" :answer/id #uuid "0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8"] ["answer-0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8" :answer/value "0.3159"] ["answer-7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7" :answer/id #uuid "7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7"] ["answer-7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7" :answer/value "0.3212"] ["answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" :answer/id #uuid "654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d"] ["answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d" :answer/value "0.3413"] ["answer-b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6" :answer/id #uuid "b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6"] ["answer-b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6" :answer/value "0.3461"] ["answer-e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd" :answer/id #uuid "e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd"] ["answer-e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd" :answer/value "0.3665"] ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/correct "answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d"] ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/choices "answer-0048806c-59ec-5b54-9603-d1aea47812e8"] ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/choices "answer-7e5e4424-4f3f-57f4-b362-4a5859daccd7"] ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/choices "answer-654e6669-1c8f-5b7c-9105-507f8fec4f7d"] ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/choices "answer-b98dc844-9500-5f8d-b857-bb196f769cd6"] ["answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1" :answer-field/choices "answer-e03cd40f-b9b7-5e51-8c05-cf33954b77fd"] ["question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-9e93d9e4-a205-5e33-a4db-5e5a5cd44ab1"] [17592186064315 :knowledge-point/questions "question-2f85c49f-b8cd-5032-99b8-bc70560e429d"] [17592186398726 :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by two units to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-2))=u(2-t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(2-t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(2-t).$"]] "retractions" [[17592186396318 :question/problem "Consider a function $b(t)$ such that its Laplace transform is $L\\{b(t)\\}=B(s)$ for $s>{s}_{0}.$ Suppose we wish to find $L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\}.$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below.By definition of the Laplace transform, we can write \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{t}b(t)\\} & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{{field-2}}\\,b(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={{field-3}}\\,\\text{for}\\,{{field-4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186411821 :answer-field/correct 17592186411689] [17592186398726 :question/worked-solution "The unit step function $u(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nu(t)={\\begin{aligned}1,\\,t\\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\,t<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=u(t)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/9f/9fb8c20672705e9d68e69d32e7ff3499ec71560e10aa85136b3f3ad75c5fb5ed.png)\n\nNotice that to transform the function $y=u(t)$ to the given function $y=f(t),$ we perform the following transformations:\n\n- First, we reflect $y=u(t)$ in the $y$-axis, which gives $y=u(-t).$\n\n- Next, we shift by two units to the right, giving $y=u(-(t-2))=u(2-t).$\n\n- Finally, we scale by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, giving $y=2u(2-t).$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $f(t)=2u(2-t).$"]]}]